xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The vendor's own layouts are pictures this program can draw.  `--theme
    Theme_07`, `{"cmd":"theme","value":"Theme_07"}` over the control socket,
    and the window's layout editor all name one file.  23 of ALPHA's 27
    layouts are selectable; the four 320x240 ones are refused on their own
    `ratio` field rather than stretched onto a 480x480 glass, and
    `--list-themes` prints the split with the reason beside it.
    `--check-theme NAME` prints what every widget of one layout would do -
    painted, skipped because no reading answers its `source`, or skipped
    because a font face is not installed - and the counts account for every
    widget in the file, so nothing vanishes uncounted.  No layout file is
    ever sent to the panel: it is drawn here and uploaded as the same `.osd`
    frame a picture of `alpha` already is.

  * All five of this project's own themes now export to that file format at
    zero differing pixels (`--export-theme NAME`), which is what the format
    is for.  Getting there meant naming two things the vendor's renderer
    never had to write down: `xigma.arcPaint`, because one arc painted with
    the vendor's convention is a different picture from the same arc painted
    with ours (measured both ways in the suite), and `xigma.colourStops`,
    because `gauge`'s ramp is a colour rule rather than a gradient somebody
    remembered.  `--list-specials` prints the five composite rows
    (`fan-figures`, `temp-values`, `temp-labels`, `dual-status`,
    `clock-readings`) that no single vendor widget stands for.

  * `bars` lays out ten tachometer slots on every box instead of compacting
    the list to whatever one machine reports, so a layout file can say where
    a row goes; the two bar conventions it needed are held to the functions
    they name.  The readings that go behind those rows were checked against
    what the drawing prints, row for row.

  * A theme you edit is a file, and a file you write shadows the vendor's.
    `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xigmalcd/themes` is read before the checkout and
    before `/usr/share/xigmalcd/themes/alpha`, so your copy wins without
    anyone unpacking a package, and `--list-themes` says which root
    answered.  The two vendor trees are never written, and the five
    drawn-from-code names cannot be saved as files: a `gauge.json` would be
    a second source of truth for `render.theme_gauge`, which is the drift
    the export design exists to avoid.  `--check-theme gauge` says that in
    words.

  * The window's layout editor.  Pick one of the 23 selectable layouts and
    it is drawn by the same code the loop uses, from a pinned fixture, so
    the picture does not move under your cursor while you edit it; point at
    a row's ink box to select it, drag it, nudge it with the arrow keys.
    Applying is live to the preview only - nothing about an edit reaches the
    panel except through the buttons that already sent frames, and the
    guarded factory reset is still never folded away.

  * A layout names its own background, and the window follows it.  Every
    selectable layout declares a clip in `background.mediaFile`; choosing
    the layout puts that clip under the preview and marks its row, and the
    line under the picture says which file it chose and from where.  It is a
    preview rule, not a wire rule: the upload button is still the only thing
    that sends a file to the panel.  One click on another row makes the
    background yours, and from then on no layout can walk over it until the
    `Use this layout's own clip` button hands it back.

  * `media_roots()` gained the vendor's theme library, so a bare clip name
    is looked up in four roots instead of two: `$XIGMA_MEDIA`, this
    checkout's `media/`, `/usr/share/xigmalcd/media`, then the library - the
    checkout's `themes/alpha/clips`, then the copy
    `xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes` installs.  That is what makes `--mp4
    Theme_07.mp4` mean the file `--list-themes` printed beside that layout:
    23 of the 23 resolve, where 2 of 23 did before.  The media roots still
    come first, so the session default is `media/Theme_25.mp4` on every box,
    and no file is uploaded that a flag did not ask for.

  * The two doors made to agree about a frame.  A generated layout and the
    code drawing it came from are compared at one pinned reading dict and
    one pinned instant - 15 of the 19 sources a layout asks for are answered
    by that one dict, and the reader's own text colour reaches a generated
    file through declared skin roles (`render.SKIN`, `themes.with_ink`)
    rather than an `ink` argument, which is what lets the ink comparisons
    land at zero differing pixels at all.

  * `--bg black` is a canvas, not a promise about the finished frame, and
    the documents said the promise.  For a layout theme the plate is
    composited *under* the finished layer, so every pixel comes out at alpha
    255 and the panel's stored background is covered.  For three of the five
    drawn themes (`alpha`, `dual`, `gauge`) it is not: a `theme_*` body
    opens its image on the canvas, PIL replaces a translucent fill instead
    of blending it, and those themes keep letting the stored background
    through their own ring tracks and plates.  Which themes leak is derived
    from the exports' colour keys by the suite, which prints the pixel
    counts beside the machine instead of quoting them.

  * `--no-clock` does what it says on the theme that is mostly a clock.
    `theme_clock` took a `clock` argument and never read it, so the flag
    moved zero pixels; it now drops the big time line and leaves the date
    and the readings line where they are.  That is a frame change on the
    glass for anyone using the flag - every frame drawn *without* it is
    byte-identical to before, and the suite measures that rather than
    asserting it.

  * Two questions that used to be argued are now measured by a tool.
    `--print-theme-fields` prints the field census over the vendor's files
    and `layout.py`'s own source: 33 keys across 152 widgets, 22 of them
    read, 11 carried by the vendor's files and never read, 4 read with no
    vendor example.  `tools/knobmap.py` answers, option by option, what a
    layout file can do in place of a `theme_*` body: 28 of the 37 (theme,
    option) pairs land at zero differing pixels and 9 do not, and the suite
    holds the floor above 900 px on any machine - which is the recorded
    reason the five stay drawn from code.

  * An installed library is read back now, not only hashed.  With the themes
    package present, `debian/tests/smoke` runs `themes.scan()` against the
    installed tree, paints every selectable layout through `themes.paint()`
    from one pinned dict at one pinned instant, holds the per-layout
    accounting identity, agrees with `--list-themes` on the same split, and
    resolves each layout's declared clip through the loop's own name lookup.
    The themes `.deb` is built, uploaded and installed on every CI run
    rather than only on a tag: before that, the whole section printed
    SKIPPED on a push and the figures rested on one bench.

  * Gates for the things that had quietly drifted.  `tools/selftest.sh`,
    twenty-two offline sections, now reads the width of debian/changelog (a
    line over 80 columns is a lintian *warning*, which fails the .deb job
    while the python suite stays green), re-derives the clip census from the
    files this repository tracks (46 mp4 files, 40 distinct contents, 6
    copies, 3 of them renamed) instead of quoting an audit of one machine's
    mounted partitions, holds the pairing rule to the layout files
    themselves, and re-prints every sentence in the documents that quotes a
    number a tool prints - including the numbers in docs/tools.md, two of
    which had gone stale a day after they were written.

 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 09:35:57 +0200
xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * xigmaui in three columns instead of one long scroll, at the author's own
    1 919x1 055: the clip list had a 190 px viewport with 114 px rows, which is
    two clips on screen, and `ResizeMode.Adjust` painted the selected row
    as a block taller than the list itself.  Rows are 66 px now (64 px
    thumbnails, `Fixed`, uniform, and every row *told* its height - uniform
    sizing latches the first row's hint before any thumbnail exists).  The clip
    library is its own column, the log sits under the preview where the height
    cannot be used for anything else, and a fresh window opens at 1180x780.
    Measured rows in view: 7 at the default, 12 at 1 919x1 055, where it was 2.

  * `advanced controls`: one checkbox, default off, folding six secondary things
    - canvas, scrim, fan figures, the display's event ring, the panel's `conn 1`
    hello, the 30 s frame run.  Pixels only, never values: Apply sends what is
    hidden.  The guarded factory reset is deliberately never folded away.

  * Thumbnails are letterboxed rather than stretched: 3 of the 43 clips the
    themes package installs are 320x240, and drawing a 4:3 frame as a square
    contradicted the `320x240` printed on the same row.

  * The text this program draws can be another colour, from all three doors:
    `--text-color` on the display's command line, `{"cmd": "text_color",
    "value": "#ff4040"}` on the control socket, and a `text colour` row in the
    window's theme box - its own palette by name, with swatches, or the colour
    dialog.  One parser, `render.parse_ink`, so the doors cannot drift.  Our own
    layer only: a vendor theme's lettering is decided inside that theme's layout
    file, and those are not all white either - 88 of the 106 text colours in the
    library's 23 480x480 layouts are white, and the suite re-derives that count
    now the claim is in the documents.  Measured off glass: an inked frame's
    painted area is unchanged, every glyph core moves to the chosen colour, and
    the ink survives the default 256-colour palette encoding within 1/255 per
    channel.  Measured on glass too: five inks over the control socket at 1 fps,
    80 frames, 0 refused, `space` unchanged the whole way, and the colours read
    as the colours sent - `data/glass/2026-11-07-102041-ink-on-glass-watch.txt`.

  * Against a display too old to know the new command - the installed 0.2
    service driven by this window, which is the author's own machine - the row
    goes inactive with the reason, Apply sends what that display does know and
    says which one it left alone, and the state line reads `text not reported by
    this display` rather than the misleading `text theme default`.

  * The preview pane plays.  A `playing` box above the picture decodes the
    background in sequence through one streaming ffmpeg (`clips.ClipPlayer`) and
    composites our layer over each frame; still mode keeps the seek-to-mid-loop
    thumbnail path, which is the right shape for one image and an order of
    magnitude worse for a loop - twelve frames out of one process took 0.03 s,
    while twelve seeks cost the better part of a second, and the seek is what
    would hitch.  The rate comes from the file: `_ffprobe_look` now asks ffprobe
    for `r_frame_rate` too, and of the 43 clips the themes package installs 23
    are 24 fps and 20 are 20 fps (17 of the 480x480 ones plus all three
    320x240), so a hard-coded 24 would play nearly half the library at the wrong
    speed.  Two clocks, measured rather than assumed: the layer redraws at
    `--preview-fps` (85.6 ms a tick, 71.8 ms of it `sensors.collect`) and the
    background plays on its own `QTimer` at the file's rate (41 ms for a 24 fps
    clip), pasting the cached layer over each decoded frame - a background tick
    is 0.54 ms (decode 1.06 ms with it, composite 0.26 ms, an identity
    480x480 resize 0.02 ms).  The first cut drove both from the preview timer,
    which made the animation's rate equal `--preview-fps`: on this bench's own
    saved `preview_fps: 1.0` that was a 1 fps slideshow, correctly reported as
    "very slow and stutters at about 1 fps", and the split takes the same window
    to 24.3 fps of background with a 1 fps layer (23.3 at 2).  Rate capped at
    30, floored at 5; a file whose rate ffprobe will not give is played at 24
    and the line under the picture says so.  A hidden window decodes nothing:
    `hideEvent` closes the ffmpeg and stops both timers, 2 s hidden shows and
    decodes zero frames, and a `black` canvas never opens a stream because no
    background shows there.  Remembered as `animate`.  The seam the viewer sees
    is the file's own and is not a splice: 23-24 frames a pass, and the
    first frame of three successive passes is byte-identical
    (f7e5ded0b703).  The two lines under the picture change with the
    state rather than at the next preview tick: a window at `preview_fps
    1.0` otherwise went on saying "one mid-loop frame" for a second
    after `playing` was ticked or the canvas switched, and the caption's
    second half had no separator before the file's own note, printing
    "...measures those.background Theme_25.mp4 at mid-loop".  Both were
    caught by the suite's new assertions - one of them as a race, which
    is the honest way to find it - and the caption now names the loop's
    own facts instead: `Theme_25.mp4 is 0.96 s at 24 fps, 480x480`.
    Measured with the layer clock stopped and the background's running:
    46 frames reached the label in 2.0 s at a 1 fps layer.

  * tools/preview_gif.py writes that composite out as a file, and README's five
    theme previews are 10-12 s MP4s now: 480 px, the background clip's own 20 or
    24 fps, 240 frames, x264 at crf 20 / preset medium, 214 113 B to
    1 930 104 B each, 5 061 583 B for the six files in docs/videos/.  Motion in
    a README has to be a GIF - GitHub turns an .mp4 written in markdown into an
    <img> no browser plays - but a 10 s GIF is several MB of the wrong codec, so
    these go up through GitHub's own editor, which rewrites the markdown to its
    CDN; README keeps a visible marker in each cell - the file to upload, and
    the instruction to delete the marker - until that upload happens.  The GIF
