  * The clip list is one row per distinct clip, not one per path.  With the
    themes package installed and the Windows partition mounted, the roots the
    window walks held 161 mp4 files for 40 clips, so it showed 161 rows for 40
    backgrounds.  Three kinds of copy: the Windows app re-copies a library clip
    into its `media/background/` under a millisecond name every time a theme is
    applied (all 26 `175749*.mp4` there are byte-identical to a `Theme_NN` or
    `theme_NN` clip, whole-file sha256); the vendor library repeats inside the
    copy this repository carries (`Theme_31.mp4` == `Theme_26.mp4`,
    `theme_38.mp4` == `Theme_27.mp4`, `theme_51.mp4` == `Theme_29.mp4`); and the
    installed packages sit next to the checkout holding the same bytes again.
    Those 26 are deliberately NOT deleted from the Windows volume: the vendor
    app's own `store.json` names 31 of them as a theme background, and a program
    that deleted them would leave that app holding paths to files that are not
    there.  So the collapse happens in this program's list, keyed on length then
    sha256 of the first and last MiB (whole file under 2 MiB) - the older
    (name, length) rule could not see a renamed copy, and left 69 rows standing.
    Within one root the better-named file wins, because timestamp names sort
    before `Theme_*` (digits before letters); across roots the precedence order
    stands, so the row shown is the file `find_media()` would have chosen.
    Measured: 161 files -> 40 rows, 121 copies dropped, 35 of them under a
    different name - and 40 is exactly what an independent audit of all 161
    files by whole-file sha256 found distinct.  The window says the numbers out
    loud in its log, because a list that quietly gets shorter looks like a bug.

  * The vendor's `.thm` containers are out of the tree and out of both packages:
    27 files, 66 300 082 B, 43.8 % of themes/alpha and roughly 40 % of the whole
    repository.  They are the vendor's own encrypted ZIP around media this copy
    already holds unpacked; nothing here reads one, and the control socket's
    file gate would not accept one.  What is kept is their identity:
    `manifest.json` gained a `notRedistributed` array (vendor path, byte count
    and sha256 for all 27) and every theme row keeps
    `thmFile`/`thmVendorPath`/`thmBytes`/`thmSha256`, so a reader holding the
    vendor's installer can check this record rather than take it on trust - and
    they are told where it comes from, since those files arrive inside
    Connect-Display-Setup-Software-2025-1125.zip from xigmatek.com.
    `tools/thm_unlock.py --no-thm` writes the tree this way; `--verify` with no
    Windows volume now prints `125/125 files match manifest hashes` where it
    printed 152/152, and given `--from DIR` it re-hashes the 27 this tree does
    not carry where they live (measured 152/152 on the author's bench).  Proof
    that nothing else moved: a `--no-thm` write re-derived every remaining file
    and all 125 kept manifest entries byte for byte, key for key, against the
    pre-removal tree.  themes/alpha is 100 files, 84 926 540 B of them the
    vendor's own bytes, and the themes .deb built from that tree measured
    79 841 286 B against 146 137 362 B at 0.2, 102 file members with 0 `.thm`
    among them, Installed-Size 83 109 KiB against 147 854 KiB; two builds of it
    twenty seconds apart were byte-identical, and lintian says 0 errors, 0
    warnings, 0 infos.  That container size has drifted since, with the library
    not moving: `changelog.gz` ships inside the themes package and this stanza
    is still UNRELEASED, so docs/packaging.md quotes the payload digest of the
    installed library instead - 46fb6f2d… over its 100 files, the same in
    every build taken since.  `debian/tests/smoke` enforces the shape by
    walking the installed tree and failing on any file the manifest does not
    name, and
    tools/selftest.sh asserts there is no .thm anywhere in the tree, that the
    27 recorded hashes agree with the per-theme rows, and the 100-file layout.

